{% schema %}
  {
    
    "name": "Section 2",
    "tag": "section",
    "class": "gps-613312589409026995 gps gpsil",
    "settings": [{"type":"paragraph","content":"Section design by GemPages. [Click here to start design](https://admin.shopify.com/store/0ggrd6-bp/apps/gempages-cro/app/shopify/edit?pageType=GP_STATIC&editorId=613312589358695347&sectionId=613312589409026995)"},{"type":"select","label":"Section Preload","id":"section_preload","options":[{"label":"On","value":"true"},{"label":"Off","value":"false"},{"label":"Off Lazy Script","value":"off_lazy_script"}],"default":"false"},{"type":"text","label":"Checksum","id":"checksum"},{"type":"html","id":"ggiRVEb60z6_text","label":"ggiRVEb60z6_text","default":"<p><span style=\"font-size:px;\">Health &amp; Wellness</span></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggDOqQu7nmY_text","label":"ggDOqQu7nmY_text","default":"<p><span style=\"font-size:px;\">Health &amp; Wellness</span></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggo8l2QWdJB_text","label":"ggo8l2QWdJB_text","default":"<span style=\"color:rgb(26,26,26);font-size:28.8px;\"><strong>Scientists Studied Why Men Over 38 Crash Every Afternoon. Most American Men Find The Reason Deeply Uncomfortable.</strong></span>"},{"type":"html","id":"gg-uAkU7T-J_text","label":"gg-uAkU7T-J_text","default":"Replace this text with your content"},{"type":"html","id":"ggsSx-yhiL4_text","label":"ggsSx-yhiL4_text","default":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#242424;font-size:px;\"><strong>By Pete Turner | Updated April 04- 20206</strong></span></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggrBpXrtRT5_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggrBpXrtRT5_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_48cvys48cvys48cv.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggrBpXrtRT5_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"ggrBpXrtRT5_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1920x1080?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggrBpXrtRT5_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggrBpXrtRT5_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_48cvys48cvys48cv.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggic8jQUQxI_text","label":"ggic8jQUQxI_text","default":"<p>Most men will never know what's actually causing their 2pm wall.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They'll blame the job. The kids. Getting older.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But the research points to something else entirely.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:#FFFFFF;color:rgb(26,26,26);font-size:16px;\"><i>The uncomfortable part isn't that you're tired. It's that the thing you rely on every single morning — the one ritual you've never once questioned — may be a significant part of the reason you are</i></span></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"gghBPukldsi_text","label":"gghBPukldsi_text","default":"<strong>What Endocrinologists Found Will Change How You Think About Your Morning Cup</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"gg0V4lIP9ZU_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"gg0V4lIP9ZU_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_lgr6tvlgr6tvlgr6.png"},{"type":"html","id":"gg0V4lIP9ZU_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"gg0V4lIP9ZU_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1376x768?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"gg0V4lIP9ZU_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"gg0V4lIP9ZU_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_lgr6tvlgr6tvlgr6.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggcaJaqEaOF_text","label":"ggcaJaqEaOF_text","default":"<p>Researchers studying testosterone decline in men aged 35–50 spent years trying to solve a puzzle.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Men whose testosterone levels were technically normal were still showing every symptom of low T.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Fatigue. Brain fog. No drive. Afternoon crashes. The slow widening gap between who they were at 32 and who was showing up every day.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The culprit wasn't age. It wasn't stress levels. It wasn't sleep quality.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>For a large percentage of men in the study, it came down to something sitting on their kitchen counter. The one thing they trusted every morning — and never once questioned.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>In a 2019 randomized controlled trial of 64 men, researchers measured salivary cortisol and free testosterone across different caffeine intake levels — controlling for sleep, diet, and activity.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The finding, consistent across multiple endocrinology studies, was stark.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Men with the highest daily caffeine intake had up to <strong>18% lower free testosterone</strong> than low-caffeine controls — despite living nearly identical lives in every other measurable way.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Not total testosterone. Free testosterone — the fraction your cells can actually use. The number that doesn't appear on most standard panels.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><i>This is the same pathway endocrinologists monitor when patients report fatigue and low drive despite normal testosterone levels. It's consistently observed across multiple peer-reviewed trials and is considered a standard marker of HPA axis dysfunction in men's health research.</i></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Now think about what that means for your own day.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The meeting you barely stayed present for. The evening you checked out before dinner was over. The drive that used to arrive on its own — and now has to be pulled out of somewhere.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>None of that was willpower. None of it was age.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was a hormonal cycle. Running quietly, every single morning. Compounding every year.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><i>It's not the 2pm crash that gets you. It's who you are by 8pm because of it.</i></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>You've felt it. There's a specific minute — somewhere between 1:30 and 2:30pm — where something shuts off. Not gradually. Almost to the clock.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>You reach for another cup. It barely helps. You already know the moment I'm describing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><i>Same coffee every morning.</i></p><p><i>Same crash every afternoon.</i></p><p><i>Same man — disappearing in the second half of every day.</i></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That's the cycle. It has a cause. And here is what changes when it stops.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>You're still sharp at 3pm. You come home and you're actually there.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>You stop managing your energy and start just having it. The second half of every day stops being something to push through and starts being something you're actually present for.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That is what's actually at stake. Not a number on a lab panel.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggmUHg1cNld_text","label":"ggmUHg1cNld_text","default":"<strong>It Has Nothing To Do With How Much Coffee You Drink.</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggoNlJauDRq_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggoNlJauDRq_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_p0wjrnp0wjrnp0wj.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggoNlJauDRq_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"ggoNlJauDRq_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1920x1080?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggoNlJauDRq_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggoNlJauDRq_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_p0wjrnp0wjrnp0wj.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggBrqTIa3tF_text","label":"ggBrqTIa3tF_text","default":"<p>If you drink 3 to 5 cups a day, something has been quietly happening to your hormones for years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Not suddenly. Not in one big moment. Invisibly.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Caffeine triggers a cortisol spike within 30 minutes of your first cup — typically 30 to 40% above baseline, measurable via salivary cortisol assay, the same test used in clinical HPA axis evaluation. Cortisol is your primary stress hormone. It temporarily masks fatigue and creates what you feel as alertness.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That feeling isn't energy you made. It's energy your body borrowed.</p><p>Here's what makes it a trap.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Cortisol and testosterone operate on a seesaw. As cortisol rises, it suppresses the HPG axis — the hormonal command chain responsible for testosterone production. Less luteinizing hormone.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Less testosterone.&nbsp;</p><p>More fatigue.</p><p>&nbsp;More coffee.</p><p>&nbsp;More cortisol.&nbsp;</p><p>Less testosterone.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Every cup you drink to fight the fatigue may be deepening the cycle that's causing it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This is why the crash happens at the same time every day. This is why cutting back on coffee doesn't fix it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;This is why the afternoon fog persists no matter how well you slept.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It isn't your sleep. It isn't your age. It's a cortisol loop — one that feeds on itself every time you open a new pod.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And this is also why nothing you've tried has fixed it. The answer was never going to come from another T-booster.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;It required something that addresses the cortisol ceiling first — something the supplement industry has very little incentive to build.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>If nothing changes</strong></p><p>This won't stay the same. The crash gets earlier. The gap gets wider.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The version of you that shows up at night gets quieter. Less present. Harder to reach.</p><p>And eventually — and this is the part most men miss — you stop noticing it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;It just becomes what 45 feels like. What 50 feels like. The new normal, accepted without question.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Testosterone decline doesn't pause while you think it over. Every week in the cycle is a week the baseline drops a little further.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggBFrssm1jl_label","label":"ggBFrssm1jl_label","default":"<p><span style=\"font-size:px;\">Check Testosterone Coffee Availability Now</span></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggyD6wuce_f_text","label":"ggyD6wuce_f_text","default":"<span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#242424;font-size:px;\"><strong>And Nothing You've Tried Has Fixed It. The Reason Is Uncomfortable.</strong></span>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggun3uM3Lko_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggun3uM3Lko_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/32b35717-1519-4628-bdb7-2676ae3a9be8.jpg"},{"type":"html","id":"ggun3uM3Lko_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"ggun3uM3Lko_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1920x1080?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggun3uM3Lko_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggun3uM3Lko_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/32b35717-1519-4628-bdb7-2676ae3a9be8.jpg"},{"type":"html","id":"ggWn_3RuWaJ_text","label":"ggWn_3RuWaJ_text","default":"<p>Most solutions attempt one of two things.</p><p>T-boosters target testosterone directly — without removing the cortisol ceiling that's suppressing it.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The hormone may rise slightly. The cortisol pushes it back. The cycle continues. The purchases repeat.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Cutting caffeine reduces the cortisol stimulus — but doesn't restore the testosterone already suppressed. Adenosine floods back.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Fatigue intensifies before it improves. Most men quit during that window, right before the recalibration could take hold.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Neither one addresses why the crash keeps coming back. Because neither one was built to.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Less than 25% of T-boosters have peer-reviewed efficacy data behind them — documented by researchers at USC's Keck School of Medicine.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Most men try everything, feel nothing, and conclude this is simply what getting older looks like.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That conclusion makes sense.</p><p>It is also, for many men, wrong.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The fix wasn't going to come from a GNC shelf. It wasn't going to come from a clinic charging $300 a month. It came from a direction nobody in the consumer market expected.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And if part of you thinks this sounds like every other supplement ad —that's fair.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The industry has earned that skepticism. What follows isn't a new ingredient. It's a different mechanism — one built around a causal chain that every other formula has skipped entirely.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggZ22Igp7iS_text","label":"ggZ22Igp7iS_text","default":"<strong>This Insight Didn't Come From A Supplement Company. It Came From Doctors Trying To Understand Why Their Patients Weren't Getting Better</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggDniwOo2h7_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggDniwOo2h7_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_u6leydu6leydu6le.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggDniwOo2h7_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggDniwOo2h7_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_5ii3445ii3445ii3.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggVjsDI2aB9_text","label":"ggVjsDI2aB9_text","default":"<p>Endocrinologists were seeing a pattern across their practices. Men on testosterone therapy — serum T rising, labs improving — were still exhausted. Still foggy. Still crashing every afternoon.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The hormone was present. It wasn't working.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The answer was cortisol. Specifically: chronic cortisol elevation was suppressing androgen receptor sensitivity — muting the cellular response to testosterone even when the hormone itself measured in normal range. The HPG axis was blunted upstream. The pituitary signal was being blocked before it could do anything downstream.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The insight that followed was straightforward — but nobody in the consumer market had acted on it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Before you can restore testosterone, you have to remove the cortisol ceiling. And if you're delivering caffeine in the same product, you have to counteract the cortisol spike caffeine creates — in the same cup, in real time, every single morning.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That is the Cortisol Override Matrix™.</p><p>Not a list of trending herbs. A three-phase mechanism built around a causal chain that had to be addressed in the right sequence to function at all.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"gghiNc_yDMZ_text","label":"gghiNc_yDMZ_text","default":"<strong>The Discovery That Changes Everything. It Works In Three Phases</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"gglCfoO7UNI_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"gglCfoO7UNI_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_ertfx0ertfx0ertf.png"},{"type":"html","id":"gglCfoO7UNI_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"gglCfoO7UNI_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1920x1080?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"gglCfoO7UNI_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"gglCfoO7UNI_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_ertfx0ertfx0ertf.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggTnp3P51IA_text","label":"ggTnp3P51IA_text","default":"<p>Every ingredient was selected for a specific mechanistic role in a specific sequence. This is how it works.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Phase 1 — Cortisol Suppression.</strong> KSM-66 Ashwagandha (600mg) directly counteracts the cortisol spike produced by the Arabica it's delivered with — in real time, in the same cup. Consistently observed across multiple 8-week randomized controlled trials, KSM-66 produced an average 27% reduction in serum cortisol. This lifts the ceiling before Phase 2 can begin.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Phase 2 — Testosterone Restoration.</strong> With the ceiling removed, five compounds address the full hormonal chain. Tongkat Ali (1,000mg) frees bound testosterone from SHBG proteins — making existing T bioavailable. Shilajit (200mg) elevates luteinizing hormone, the upstream production trigger documented in <i>Andrologia</i>. Black Maca (1,000mg) supports HPG axis neurological signaling. Fenugreek inhibits aromatase conversion. Tribulus supports androgen receptor sensitivity.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Phase 3 — Clean Energy Delivery.</strong></p><p>&nbsp;Premium Arabica (8,000mg) paired with L-Theanine (200mg) at the research-backed ratio eliminates the spike-and-crash cycle. L-Citrulline (6,000mg) — matching the clinical sports performance dose — drives nitric oxide production for sustained physical and cognitive output without additional cortisol load.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>No new routine. No quitting caffeine. No pills to remember. No protocol to follow.<br><br>You're already making coffee tomorrow. This just replaces what's in the cup.<br><br>Wet the scoop. Mix it in your mug. Drink it like you always do. That is it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><i>Same ritual. Same time.&nbsp;</i></p><p><i>Same mug.</i></p><p><i>Different mechanism.&nbsp;</i></p><p><i>Different second half of every day.</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggRiGijWx9P_text","label":"ggRiGijWx9P_text","default":"<strong>What Actually Changes When The Cycle Is Fixed</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggRZMFzBw04_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggRZMFzBw04_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/085b14b3-1d29-4920-812b-7eb80a3a80d4.jpg"},{"type":"html","id":"ggRZMFzBw04_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"ggRZMFzBw04_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1600x900?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggRZMFzBw04_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggRZMFzBw04_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/085b14b3-1d29-4920-812b-7eb80a3a80d4.jpg"},{"type":"html","id":"ggTBsAVET1O_text","label":"ggTBsAVET1O_text","default":"<p>Nobody talks about this part.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Not the lab numbers. What fixing the cycle actually changes day to day.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Around day nine, something shifts.</p><p>You're at your desk at 2:30pm. You notice you haven't thought about coffee since 10am. You're not crashing.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>You're not fighting. You're just — in it. Still working. The absence of the wall is so unfamiliar it takes a moment to recognize what it means.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>By week three, your partner notices before you say anything.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>\"You seem different.\"</p><p>\"Different how?\"</p><p>\"Just — present. I don't know.\"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That is the moment. Not a lab result. Not a before and after photo. A real person who sees you every day noticing something they can't quite name — because the version of you they'd gotten used to has quietly been replaced by an earlier one.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This isn't about caffeine. It's about getting your edge back.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The version of you that didn't need to fight through the second half of every day. The one who came home and was actually there — with something left.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That is what actually changes. Not a number. You.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggcaeLkUcG9_text","label":"ggcaeLkUcG9_text","default":"<strong>What Happened When Men Who Had Given Up Tried It Anyway</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggI4jdCtr2h_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggI4jdCtr2h_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_ys02blys02blys02.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggI4jdCtr2h_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"ggI4jdCtr2h_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1920x1080?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggI4jdCtr2h_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggI4jdCtr2h_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Gemini_Generated_Image_ys02blys02blys02.png"},{"type":"html","id":"gg9YS7FSj-0_text","label":"gg9YS7FSj-0_text","default":"<p><strong>Ryan K., 43, Georgia.</strong></p><p><i>\"I tracked it. 12 days straight without a 2pm crash. I kept a note on my phone because I didn't believe it at first. That streak hasn't happened since my early 30s. I'm 43.\"</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggdEc5cncqt_text","label":"ggdEc5cncqt_text","default":"<p><strong>Derek M., 41, Ohio.</strong></p><p><i>\"Skipped my second coffee on day nine without noticing. First time in six years. My wife asked why I hadn't made one. I genuinely hadn't thought about it.\"</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggSvcjPKL9b_text","label":"ggSvcjPKL9b_text","default":"<p><strong>James R., 38, Tennessee.</strong></p><p><i>\"My doctor asked what I'd changed at my checkup. Same diet, same sleep, same everything. Just the morning cup. He told me whatever I was doing, keep doing it.\"</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"gg5na4YXErA_text","label":"gg5na4YXErA_text","default":"<p><strong>Chris W., 44, North Carolina.</strong></p><p><i>\"Four T-boosters in two years. None of them touched the actual crash. This one makes sense on a mechanical level. And it works the same way — mechanically.</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggToPZJWETf_text","label":"ggToPZJWETf_text","default":"<p><strong>Marcus T., 39, Indiana.</strong></p><p><i>\"I used to check out at 8pm. Kids would try to show me something and I had nothing left. That's gone now. That part I didn't expect — and it's the one that actually matters.\"</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggJsErURFu2_text","label":"ggJsErURFu2_text","default":"<p><strong>Brian L., 46, Texas.</strong></p><p><i>\"I basically accepted that this was just what 46 felt like. I was wrong.\"</i></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggWx_xZOTiD_text","label":"ggWx_xZOTiD_text","default":"<strong>&nbsp;Here Is How To Avoid It The Mistake Most Men Make</strong>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggsAIir8wDW_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggsAIir8wDW_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Coffee_3_6b376c39-395d-4945-b8c0-2c8e43ca7e59.jpg"},{"type":"html","id":"ggsAIir8wDW_srcSet_tablet_src","label":"ggsAIir8wDW_srcSet_tablet_src","default":"https://placehold.co/1376x768?text=Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggsAIir8wDW_srcSet_mobile_src","label":"ggsAIir8wDW_srcSet_mobile_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0653/0908/1645/files/Coffee_3_6b376c39-395d-4945-b8c0-2c8e43ca7e59